  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of the typical jersey numbers for **linebackers** in the NFL:\n\n### Current NFL Rules (Post-2021 Expansion)\n\nLinebackers are currently permitted to wear numbers **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### Most Traditional/Common Ranges\n\n- **50s (50\u201359):** Numbers 50 to 59 are traditionally worn by linebackers and centers. This has historically been the most iconic range for linebackers.\n\n- **90s (90\u201399):** Numbers 90 to 99 are also worn by linebackers and defensive linemen. The NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the 90\u201399 range in 1984, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers.\n\n---\n\n### Historical Evolution\n\n- Prior to 1984, linebackers were only allowed to wear numbers in the 50\u201359 range.\n- The NFL Competition Committee made a change in 2015, allowing linebackers to use numbers 40 through 49 for the first time.\n- In 2021, NFL owners passed a rule further expanding available numbers, allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379 and 90\u201399** \u2014 though the current official rule as listed by NFL Football Operations caps it at **1\u201359 and 90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Summary Table\n\n| Range | Notes |\n|-------|-------|\n| **1\u201359** | Now allowed (most common: 50s) |\n| **90\u201399** | Also allowed; shared with D-linemen |\n| **40\u201349** | Allowed since 2015 |\n\nThe **50s** remain the most classic and recognizable numbers for linebackers, with many all-time greats having worn numbers in that range.",
